;; ============================================================
;; The fixpoint driver  —  SPEC.md §6.5, §6.8
;;
;; GROWL's `engine-run` is a semi-naive fixpoint: apply rule groups to
;; the delta, collect new facts, dedup, iterate until the delta is
;; empty (bounded by max-iterations, cancellable via cancel-ptr).
;; HOWL's saturation is THE SAME LOOP with Fact = Derived instead of
;; Fact = Triple, and one structural difference: GROWL's delta is a
;; single global set, while HOWL's is SHARDED ACROSS CONTEXTS. "The
;; delta is empty" becomes "`active` is empty" — no context has
;; anything in its inbox. The loop shape is unchanged; what it
;; iterates over is not.
;;
;; ROUND SEMANTICS ARE BINDING, NOT AN IMPLEMENTATION CHOICE (§6.8).
;; The driver advances in semi-naive rounds with a barrier: every
;; context processes the messages it held at the start of round n, and
;; messages produced during round n are delivered for round n+1. Round
;; count is therefore a function of the input alone, so max-iterations
;; cuts at the same point on every host at any worker count. Within a
;; round, work distribution stays free.
;;
;;   Sₙ   := Sₙ₋₁ ∪ Δₙ          freeze at round start, INCLUDING
;;                              this round's arrivals
;;   for δ ∈ Δₙ: join δ against Sₙ      so Δₙ × Δₙ is covered too
;;   Δₙ₊₁ := conclusions \ Sₙ   NOVEL facts only
;;   active := contexts with a non-empty Δₙ₊₁
;;
;; ============================================================
;; HOW THE FREEZE IS ACHIEVED: Δₙ IS COMMITTED AT THE *PREVIOUS*
;; BARRIER, SO NOTHING WRITES THE STORE DURING A ROUND.
;;
;; This is the load-bearing design decision in the file, and it is
;; forced by SLOP's data representation rather than chosen for
;; elegance. `(Set T)` and `(Map K V)` compile to `slop_map*` —
;; POINTERS — so a `Context` read out of `sat.contexts` shares its sets
;; with the stored one. The obvious implementation, which folds
;; conclusions into `subsumers` as they are produced, therefore ALIASES
;; the frozen snapshot Sₙ with the live store: a fact derived early in
;; round n becomes instantly visible to rules still running in round n.
;; That is exactly what §6.4 forbids, and here it is the DEFAULT
;; BEHAVIOUR rather than an easy mistake to make.
;;
;; Two independent runtime hazards forbid mutate-as-you-go anyway:
;; `slop_map_put` rehashes at 75% load while `for-each` re-reads `cap`
;; each iteration (so mutating a set under iteration silently skips or
;; revisits elements), and `slop_map_remove` clears `occupied` with no
;; tombstone in a linear-probe table (so a removal can strand later
;; members of a probe chain — hence `active` is REBUILT, never pruned).
;;
;; The resolution: commit Δₙ into the store at the end of round n−1.
;; Then during round n the store simply IS Sₙ = Sₙ₋₁ ∪ Δₙ, with no
;; snapshot copy at all, and it is frozen because nothing writes it.
;; Δₙ travels separately in `Saturation.queues`, so rules still know
;; which facts are new. A round is then:
;;
;;   round-join    STRICTLY READ-ONLY over `sat`, emits into RoundDelta
;;   commit-round  THE BARRIER — the only mutation site in this file,
;;                 partitioned so that each context has one writer
;;
;; Two alternatives were rejected. COPYING the subsumer sets at round
;; start needs the whole store copied every round to be correct
;; (O(rounds × |store|)), which will not meet the 5×-ELK budget.
;; TWO-GENERATION CONTEXTS (a `pending` set beside `subsumers`) add a
;; second `slop_map*` with identical aliasing character rather than
;; removing the first, and let `extract-findings` read the uncommitted
;; generation by mistake — a silent over-report with no error.
;;
;; HOW THE INVARIANT IS HELD. It reduces to one reviewable property:
;; `round-join` and everything it calls contain no `set-put`/`map-put`/
;; `list-push` whose target is reachable from `sat`. Until slop #278 it
;; was also a compile error, because a put needed an arena in scope and
;; the read-only accessors below take none. Since #278 a put grows the
;; collection in the arena it was made in and needs nothing in scope, so
;; that guard is GONE: a store write in the join phase now compiles. What
;; catches one is `make test-tsan`, which runs every test's round on
;; worker threads, so a join that writes the shared store races with its
;; siblings and is reported.
;;
;; TERMINATION. `A ⊑ B, B ⊑ A`: round 1 admits both against the empty
;; store and commits them; round 2 re-derives both, `admit` finds them
;; already stored, nothing is touched, the frontier empties, fixpoint.
;; This is §6.4's `\ Sₙ` against the ACCUMULATED store — which works
;; precisely because the store absorbed Δₙ at the previous barrier.
;; Without it an ordinary cyclic hierarchy burns its budget forever.
;;
;; DETERMINISM. A round is a read-only fold producing a SET-VALUED
;; Δₙ₊₁, committed by set union — commutative and idempotent, so the
;; content of Sₙ₊₁, the touched set, and therefore the round count do
;; not depend on visitation order. Set iteration order is bucket order
;; and is NOT content-canonical, so queue list order must never escape
;; into a report; it does not, because `classify.extract-findings` is
;; the only path out and sorts (§6.7). That chain is what licenses
;; "byte-identical across worker counts" without a canonical set order
;; this runtime cannot provide.
;; ============================================================

(module saturate
  (@doc "Semi-naive barrier-synchronized saturation driver")

  (import types (Node RoleId NormAxiom Derived Addressed LogicalEdge
                 Context Queue Saturation
                 Termination Fault ReasonerConfig
                 node-top node-bottom make-context make-queue cancel-requested))
  (import el (apply-el-rules))
  (import premise (RuleIndex))
  (import thread (spawn join))

  (export
    RoundDelta
    RoundResult
    saturate
    advance-round
    commit-round
    make-initial-saturation
    budget-exhausted
    frontier-is-empty
    context-of
    queue-of
    deliver-seed
    store-has-sub
    store-has-succ
    reuse-arena)

  ;; The driver returns termination alongside the state, never a bare
  ;; Saturation (§6.5): a capped run and a coherent ontology are
  ;; indistinguishable at the Saturation level, and conflating them is
  ;; how a validator returns a false pass.
  (type RoundResult (record
    (saturation  Saturation)
    (termination Termination)))

  ;; Δₙ₊₁ under construction: for each touched node, the NEW facts only.
  ;;
  ;; Reusing `Context` as the payload is deliberate — the pending facts
  ;; have exactly the shape of the facts they will be merged into, so
  ;; commit is three parallel loops with no translation step, and the
  ;; whole structure is built from `(Map Node Context)`, the one nesting
  ;; already exercised by `Saturation`. It is DRIVER-OWNED and never
  ;; stored in `Saturation`, which is what keeps it distinct from the
  ;; rejected two-generation design: nothing downstream can read it.
  ;;
  ;; The touched set is the key set of `pending` — `for-each` over a Map
  ;; iterates keys, so a separate `touched` field would be a second copy
  ;; of the same information, free to drift.
  (type RoundDelta (record
    (pending (Map Node Context))))

  ;; ============================================================
  ;; READ-ONLY STORE ACCESSORS
  ;;
  ;; NO `Arena` PARAMETER, and nothing here may mutate: the join reads the
  ;; frozen store through these. Before slop #278 the missing arena made a
  ;; store write here a compile error; since #278 it would compile, so the
  ;; frozen-store invariant rests on review and on `make test-tsan` (see
  ;; the header).
  ;; ============================================================

  (fn context-of ((sat Saturation) (n Node))
    (@intent "The stored context for a node, if the registry has one")
    (@spec ((Saturation Node) -> (Option Context)))
    (@pure)
    (map-get (. sat contexts) n))

  (fn queue-of ((sat Saturation) (n Node))
    (@intent "Δₙ for a node — the messages it must process this round")
    (@spec ((Saturation Node) -> (Option Queue)))
    (@pure)
    (map-get (. sat queues) n))

  (fn store-has-sub ((sat Saturation) (x Node) (b Node))
    (@intent "Is B ∈ S(X) already in the accumulated store?")
    (@spec ((Saturation Node Node) -> Bool))
    (@pure)
    (match (map-get (. sat contexts) x)
      ((some ctx) (set-has (. ctx subsumers) b))
      ((none)     false)))

  (fn store-has-succ ((sat Saturation) (x Node) (r RoleId) (y Node))
    (@intent "Is the logical edge (X,Y) ∈ R(r) already stored?")
    (@spec ((Saturation Node RoleId Node) -> Bool))
    (@pure)
    ;; Tested on the SUCCESSOR side only, and that is sufficient rather
    ;; than sloppy: `round-commit` never writes a successor half without
    ;; its predecessor half, so `succ(X,r,Y) ∈ store ⟺ pred(Y,r,X) ∈
    ;; store` is a store invariant maintained by construction — the same
    ;; guarantee `EdgePair` provides at the point of creation.
    (match (map-get (. sat contexts) x)
      ((some ctx)
        (match (map-get (. ctx succs) r)
          ((some ys) (set-has ys y))
          ((none)    false)))
      ((none) false)))

  ;; ============================================================
  ;; NOVELTY — the `\ Sₙ` test, at ONE site
  ;;
  ;; Not inside the rules: CR4 runs in Y and emits to X, so a rule
  ;; cannot see its conclusion's destination context, and a store read
  ;; there would poison the per-rule faithfulness contracts. Not at
  ;; commit either: testing there still terminates, but every context
  ;; that derived nothing new would land in `touched` and the frontier
  ;; would be wrong.
  ;; ============================================================

  (fn delta-context ((arena Arena) (delta RoundDelta) (x Node))
    (@intent "The pending-facts context for x, created on first touch")
    (@spec ((Arena RoundDelta Node) -> Context))
    (@alloc arena)
    (match (map-get (. delta pending) x)
      ((some c) c)
      ((none)
        (let ((c (make-context arena x)))
          (do
            ;; `map-put` stores a COPY of the record — but every field of
            ;; a Context is a `slop_map*`, so the copy shares its sets
            ;; with `c` and later mutations through either are the same
            ;; mutation. That uniformity is why `queue` was moved out of
            ;; `Context` (types.slop).
            (map-put (. delta pending) x c)
            c)))))

  ;; `succs`/`preds` are `(Map RoleId (Set Node))`, and neither a Map nor
  ;; a Set may appear in a function signature — the checker has no type
  ;; for them there, and nothing in the toolchain, GROWL or slop-rdf
  ;; passes one. So the per-role insert is written twice against
  ;; `Context` rather than once against the map. Two small functions
  ;; beat one that cannot be declared.
  (fn add-succ ((arena Arena) (ctx Context) (r RoleId) (y Node))
    (@intent "Record y as an r-successor of this context, creating the filler set if needed")
    (@spec ((Arena Context RoleId Node) -> Unit))
    (@alloc arena)
    (match (map-get (. ctx succs) r)
      ((some s) (set-put s y))
      ((none)
        (let ((s (set-new arena Node)))
          (do (set-put s y) (map-put (. ctx succs) r s))))))

  (fn add-pred ((arena Arena) (ctx Context) (r RoleId) (x Node))
    (@intent "Record x as an r-predecessor of this context, creating the set if needed")
    (@spec ((Arena Context RoleId Node) -> Unit))
    (@alloc arena)
    (match (map-get (. ctx preds) r)
      ((some s) (set-put s x))
      ((none)
        (let ((s (set-new arena Node)))
          (do (set-put s x) (map-put (. ctx preds) r s))))))

  (fn admit-sub ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (delta RoundDelta) (x Node) (b Node))
    (@intent "Admit B ∈ S(X) into Δₙ₊₁ if it is not already in Sₙ")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation RoundDelta Node Node) -> Unit))
    (@alloc arena)
    (when (not (store-has-sub sat x b))
      (let ((dc (delta-context arena delta x)))
        (set-put (. dc subsumers) b))))

  (fn admit-edge ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (delta RoundDelta) (e LogicalEdge))
    (@intent "Admit the LOGICAL edge, both halves or neither")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation RoundDelta LogicalEdge) -> Unit))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; §6.4: novelty is tested on the logical edge BEFORE `emit-edge`
    ;; expands it into two halves. Testing the halves separately lets a
    ;; half already present in one context re-enter because the other
    ;; was novel; requiring BOTH novel is also wrong, since it would
    ;; refuse to repair a half-written edge. One test, both writes.
    (when (not (store-has-succ sat (. e from) (. e role) (. e to)))
      (let ((dx (delta-context arena delta (. e from)))
            (dy (delta-context arena delta (. e to)))
            (r  (. e role))
            (x  (. e from))
            (y  (. e to)))
        (do
          (add-succ arena dx r y)
          (add-pred arena dy r x)))))

  (fn admit ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (delta RoundDelta) (m Addressed))
    (@intent "Route one emitted conclusion through the novelty test")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation RoundDelta Addressed) -> Unit))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; ONE LOGICAL EDGE, ADMITTED ONCE, through its successor half. A
    ;; `derived-succ (r,y)` addressed to x IS the edge (x,r,y), and
    ;; `admit-edge` tests and writes BOTH halves from it. The predecessor
    ;; half says the same thing again, and every one that reaches here has
    ;; its successor sibling in the same rule output: the only constructor
    ;; of either half on this path is `emit-edge`, whose EdgePair carries
    ;; both, and CR3 and CR7 push both (seeds never pass through
    ;; `admit`). Admitting the predecessor half too tested and wrote every
    ;; new edge twice - 1.8M redundant admits on EL-GALEN.
    (match (. m what)
      ((derived-sub b)
        (admit-sub arena sat delta (. m to) b))
      ((derived-succ r y)
        (admit-edge arena sat delta
          (record-new LogicalEdge (from (. m to)) (role r) (to y))))
      ((derived-pred _ _) (do))))

  ;; ============================================================
  ;; THE ROUND
  ;; ============================================================

  (fn round-join ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (idx RuleIndex) (delta RoundDelta))
    (@intent "Join every δ ∈ Δₙ against the frozen Sₙ, accumulating novel conclusions")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation RuleIndex RoundDelta) -> Unit))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; READ-ONLY OVER `sat`. Every write below targets `delta`. If this
    ;; function ever grows a `set-put`/`map-put`/`list-push` against
    ;; anything reachable from `sat`, the freeze is broken and the
    ;; determinism guarantee goes with it.
    (let ((ra (arena-new 1048576)))
      (do
        (for-each (n (. sat active))
          (join-context arena ra sat idx delta n))
        (arena-free ra))))

  (fn round-join-part ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (idx RuleIndex) (delta RoundDelta)
                       (part (List Node)))
    (@intent "The join over one worker's share of the frontier")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation RuleIndex RoundDelta (List Node)) -> Int))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; A WORKER'S WHOLE JOB, and exactly `round-join` over a subset: the
    ;; same per-context body, the same read-only discipline. `arena` is the
    ;; worker's OWN arena and `delta` its own RoundDelta, so the only writes
    ;; a worker makes land in memory no other thread touches; everything
    ;; else it reads — the store, the queues, the premise index — is frozen
    ;; for the whole round. Returns an Int because a spawned function is
    ;; called through `int64_t(*)(void*)`.
    (let ((ra (arena-new 1048576)))
      (do
        (for-each (n part)
          (join-context arena ra sat idx delta n))
        (arena-free ra)
        0)))

  (fn reuse-arena ((a Arena))
    (@intent "Empty an arena for reuse: its first block stays, any chained blocks are freed")
    (@spec ((Arena) -> Unit))
    ;; slop has `arena-new` and `arena-free` but no reset; the runtime's
    ;; `slop_arena_reset` is it. `a` is the C parameter name.
    (c-inline "slop_arena_reset(a);"))

  (fn join-context ((arena Arena) (ra Arena) (sat Saturation) (idx RuleIndex) (delta RoundDelta) (n Node))
    (@intent "Join one context's Δₙ against the frozen Sₙ")
    (@spec ((Arena Arena Saturation RuleIndex RoundDelta Node) -> Unit))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; THE RULES' RESULT LISTS LIVE ONE CONTEXT. They are dead once
    ;; admitted - `admit` copies each conclusion into the delta, in `arena` -
    ;; but grown in the round's arena they stayed to the barrier: most of a
    ;; round's scratch, ~300 MB in GO's busiest round, and at W = 4 a peak
    ;; that moved with how the workers' joins overlapped. So the rules build
    ;; them in `ra`, the worker's rule arena, emptied once this context is
    ;; done. One arena per worker, reset rather than made per context: a
    ;; fresh arena per context cost GO a quarter of its reasoning time.
    (match (context-of sat n)
        ((some ctx)
          (match (queue-of sat n)
            ((some q)
              ;; `ctx` is the ROUND SNAPSHOT Sₙ = Sₙ₋₁ ∪ Δₙ: the store
              ;; already absorbed Δₙ at the previous barrier, so the
              ;; Δₙ × Δₙ joins CR2 and CR7 depend on are reachable here.
              ;; The whole premise index goes in, not one axiom at a
              ;; time: CR5 has no axiom premise, so a per-axiom
              ;; dispatcher would emit |axioms| copies of every
              ;; ⊥-propagation. The dispatcher owns that distinction,
              ;; and the index is read-only for the whole run.
              (do
                (for-each (d (. q items))
                  (for-each (m (apply-el-rules ra ctx d idx))
                    (admit arena sat delta m)))
                (reuse-arena ra)))
            ((none) (do))))
        ((none) (do))))

  (fn ensure-context ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (n Node))
    (@intent "The stored context for a node, created if the registry lacks one")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation Node) -> Context))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; Only ever called from `round-commit`, and never while iterating
    ;; `sat.contexts` — `map-put` rehashes at 75% load, and a `for-each`
    ;; re-reads `cap` every iteration.
    (match (map-get (. sat contexts) n)
      ((some c) c)
      ((none)
        (let ((c (make-context arena n)))
          (do (map-put (. sat contexts) n c) c)))))

  ;; ============================================================
  ;; THE BARRIER — PARTITION, COMMIT, COLLECT (M1 slice 6b C1)
  ;;
  ;; The deltas of a round (one per join worker) are committed without
  ;; being merged first. A merge re-put every conclusion into one delta on
  ;; the coordinator, and then the commit put every one again into the
  ;; store, both serially: on EL-GALEN at four workers the two were 68% of
  ;; the round. Now:
  ;;
  ;;   partition  serial, over delta KEYS: each touched context gets one
  ;;              owner (round-robin, first seen), and every context a key
  ;;              names is created HERE, so nothing inserts into
  ;;              `sat.contexts` while committers run;
  ;;   commit     one worker per owner: it writes only the contexts it
  ;;              owns, from ALL the deltas (frozen, read-only), growing
  ;;              their sets in its OWN arena, and queues a fact only if it
  ;;              was new to the store - the de-duplication the merge did;
  ;;   collect    serial, per touched context: the owners' queues become
  ;;              the next round's queues, frontier and count.
  ;;
  ;; WHY THE ROUND IS THE SAME AT EVERY W. Each fact belongs to one
  ;; context and each context to one committer, so the store after the
  ;; barrier is the same union of novel facts; each queue holds exactly
  ;; the facts that were new, once; the frontier is the touched set. Queue
  ;; ORDER may differ, and Δ is a set (header, DETERMINISM).
  ;; ============================================================

  ;; One touched context, as one delta has it.
  (type Touched (record
    (node    Node)
    (pending Context)))

  ;; A record around the list, for `Queue`'s and `AxList`'s reason: a
  ;; `(List T)` is a value struct, so "read it, push, write it back".
  (type Bucket (record
    (items (List Touched))))

  ;; One committer's next-round queues. A record, because a Map cannot be
  ;; a parameter.
  (type Queues (record
    (by-node (Map Node Queue))))

  (fn bucketed ((arena Arena) (found (Option Bucket)) (e Touched))
    (@intent "The bucket a lookup found, with one more entry - or a new bucket")
    (@spec ((Arena (Option Bucket) Touched) -> Bucket))
    (@alloc arena)
    (let ((mut items (match found
                       ((some b) (. b items))
                       ((none)   (list-new arena Touched)))))
      (do
        (list-push items e)
        (record-new Bucket (items items)))))

  ;; The partition's working state: who owns each touched context, and
  ;; each owner's bucket.
  (type Partition (record
    (owner    (Map Node Int))
    (by-owner (Map Int Bucket))))

  (fn partition-one ((arena Arena) (run Arena) (sat Saturation) (part Partition)
                     (x Node) (dc Context) (turn Int) (w Int))
    (@intent "File one touched context under its owner, creating it if needed; return the next turn")
    (@spec ((Arena Arena Saturation Partition Node Context Int Int) -> Int))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; A function of its own because, when this was written, `x` and `dc`
    ;; had to be PARAMETERS: bound by a map `for-each`'s destructuring, the
    ;; transpiler could not type a `map-get` on them (fixed in slop #216).
    (do
      (ensure-context run sat x)
      (match (map-get (. part owner) x)
        ((some o)
          (let ((e (record-new Touched (node x) (pending dc)))
                (b (bucketed arena (map-get (. part by-owner) o) e)))
            (do (map-put (. part by-owner) o b) turn)))
        ((none)
          (let ((k (% turn w))
                (e (record-new Touched (node x) (pending dc)))
                (b (bucketed arena (map-get (. part by-owner) k) e)))
            (do
              (map-put (. part owner) x k)
              (map-put (. part by-owner) k b)
              (+ turn 1)))))))

  (fn partition-delta ((arena Arena) (run Arena) (sat Saturation) (d RoundDelta) (part Partition)
                       (turn Int) (w Int))
    (@intent "File one delta's touched contexts under their owners; return the next round-robin turn")
    (@spec ((Arena Arena Saturation RoundDelta Partition Int Int) -> Int))
    (@alloc arena)
    (let ((mut t turn))
      (do
        (for-each ((x dc) (. d pending))
          (set! t (partition-one arena run sat part x dc t w)))
        t)))

  (fn bucket-items ((arena Arena) (found (Option Bucket)))
    (@intent "An owner's entries, or none")
    (@spec ((Arena (Option Bucket)) -> (List Touched)))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; A function of its own so the lookup's type is its parameter's: a
    ;; `map-get` on a record field, matched inside a `let` binding, was
    ;; typed `void*` by the transpiler until slop #216.
    (match found
      ((some b) (. b items))
      ((none)   (list-new arena Touched))))

  (fn partition-round ((arena Arena) (run Arena) (sat Saturation) (deltas (List RoundDelta)) (w Int))
    (@intent "Give every touched context one owner in 0..w-1, creating any context a delta names")
    (@spec ((Arena Arena Saturation (List RoundDelta) Int) -> Partition))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; `arena` is the round's scratch; contexts are created in `run`, the
    ;; store's arena, exactly as `ensure-context` always did - but here,
    ;; serially, before the committers start. Round-robin in first-seen
    ;; order is deterministic for a given input and W, and balanced; the
    ;; same node from several deltas always lands in the same bucket.
    (let ((part (record-new Partition
                  (owner    (map-new arena Node Int))
                  (by-owner (map-new arena Int Bucket))))
          (mut turn 0))
      (do
        (for-each (d deltas)
          (set! turn (partition-delta arena run sat d part turn w)))
        part)))

  ;; A COMMITTER GROWS COLLECTIONS ANOTHER THREAD MADE. A context's sets
  ;; and maps were made in the run arena, but each committer grows those of
  ;; the contexts it owns, alongside the others; a collection grows in the
  ;; arena it was made in (slop #278) unless the put names one, so every put
  ;; here names `arena`, the committer's own store arena (`ca`).
  (fn commit-succ ((arena Arena) (store Context) (r RoleId) (y Node))
    (@intent "Store y as an r-successor; true when it was not already stored")
    (@spec ((Arena Context RoleId Node) -> Bool))
    (@alloc arena)
    (match (map-get (. store succs) r)
      ((some s)
        (if (set-has s y)
          false
          (do (set-put s y :arena arena) true)))
      ((none)
        (let ((s (set-new arena Node)))
          (do (set-put s y :arena arena) (map-put (. store succs) r s :arena arena) true)))))

  (fn commit-pred ((arena Arena) (store Context) (r RoleId) (x Node))
    (@intent "Store x as an r-predecessor; true when it was not already stored")
    (@spec ((Arena Context RoleId Node) -> Bool))
    (@alloc arena)
    (match (map-get (. store preds) r)
      ((some s)
        (if (set-has s x)
          false
          (do (set-put s x :arena arena) true)))
      ((none)
        (let ((s (set-new arena Node)))
          (do (set-put s x :arena arena) (map-put (. store preds) r s :arena arena) true)))))

  (fn commit-sub ((arena Arena) (store Context) (b Node))
    (@intent "Add b to S(X); true if it was new")
    (@spec ((Arena Context Node) -> Bool))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; A function of its own so the set grows in the arena passed here -
    ;; the committer's store arena - and not in the one `commit-entry`
    ;; names `arena`, its queue arena.
    (if (set-has (. store subsumers) b)
      false
      (do (set-put (. store subsumers) b :arena arena) true)))

  (fn commit-entry ((arena Arena) (ca Arena) (sat Saturation) (e Touched) (qs Queues))
    (@intent "Fold one delta's facts for one owned context into the store, queueing the new ones")
    (@spec ((Arena Arena Saturation Touched Queues) -> Unit))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; TWO ARENAS, both this committer's own. The store's sets for the
    ;; contexts it owns grow in `ca`, for the rest of the run (through
    ;; `commit-sub`, `commit-succ`, `commit-pred`, which take it). The
    ;; round's queues grow in `arena`, a queue arena freed once the next
    ;; round has read them. `sat.contexts` is only READ: `partition-round`
    ;; created every context an entry names.
    (let ((x (. e node))
          (dc (. e pending)))
      (match (map-get (. sat contexts) x)
        ((some store)
          (let ((q (match (map-get (. qs by-node) x)
                     ((some q0) q0)
                     ((none)    (make-queue arena)))))
            (do
              (for-each (b (. dc subsumers))
                (when (commit-sub ca store b)
                  (list-push (. q items) (union-new Derived derived-sub b))))
              (for-each ((r ys) (. dc succs))
                (for-each (y ys)
                  (when (commit-succ ca store r y)
                    (list-push (. q items) (union-new Derived derived-succ r y)))))
              (for-each ((r ps) (. dc preds))
                (for-each (p ps)
                  (when (commit-pred ca store r p)
                    (list-push (. q items) (union-new Derived derived-pred r p)))))
              ;; Written back AFTER every push: `q` is a value struct, and
              ;; storing it earlier would keep a copy of the wrong length.
              ;; `qs.by-node` is the coordinator's, made in its scratch; this
              ;; committer grows it in its own queue arena (slop #278).
              (map-put (. qs by-node) x q :arena arena))))
        ((none) (do)))))

  (fn commit-bucket ((arena Arena) (ca Arena) (sat Saturation) (entries (List Touched)) (qs Queues))
    (@intent "One committer's whole job: every entry of the contexts it owns")
    (@spec ((Arena Arena Saturation (List Touched) Queues) -> Int))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; Returns an Int because a spawned function is called through
    ;; `int64_t(*)(void*)`. `arena` is the committer's queue arena, `ca`
    ;; its store arena (commit-entry).
    (do
      (for-each (e entries)
        (commit-entry arena ca sat e qs))
      0))

  (fn commit-round ((arena Arena) (run Arena) (scratch Arena) (sat Saturation) (deltas (List RoundDelta))
                    (cas (List Arena)) (qas (List Arena)))
    (@intent "The barrier: fold every delta into the store, one committer per context, and rebuild the frontier")
    (@spec ((Arena Arena Arena Saturation (List RoundDelta) (List Arena) (List Arena)) -> Saturation))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; Exactly one round per call. The round COUNT is what
    ;; max-iterations cuts on, and it must be a function of the input
    ;; alone — a round advancing the counter by anything other than one
    ;; would make the cap depend on how work happened to be batched, and
    ;; with it the verdict.
    (@post {(. $result iteration) == ((. sat iteration) + 1)})
    (@post {(. $result iteration) > (. sat iteration)})
    ;; `active-count` is redundant with `active`, so it is pinned here
    ;; rather than trusted: both are written by the single collect loop.
    (@post {(. $result active-count) >= 0})
    ;;
    ;; `cas` holds one arena per committer: at W = 1 the run arena itself
    ;; and nothing is spawned, the sequential reference path; at W > 1 the
    ;; long-lived commit arenas `saturate` made for this run, which the
    ;; store grows into and extraction later reads.
    ;;
    ;; THIS ROUND'S QUEUES LIVE ONE ROUND. `arena` is the round's GENERATION
    ;; arena: the new queue and frontier maps are built in it. `qas` holds
    ;; one queue arena per committer (at W = 1, `arena` itself), where each
    ;; committer's queues grow. The next round's join reads them, and then
    ;; they are dead: `saturate` frees a generation once the round after it
    ;; has committed. `run` is the run arena, where `partition-round`
    ;; creates any context a delta names.
    (let ((w       (cast Int (list-len cas)))
          (buckets (partition-round scratch run sat deltas w))
          (mut qss     (list-new scratch Queues))
          (mut threads (list-new scratch (Ptr (Thread Int))))
          (mut k 0))
      (do
        (while (< k w)
          (let ((qs (record-new Queues (by-node (map-new scratch Node Queue))))
                (ca (match (list-get cas k) ((some a) a) ((none) run)))
                (qa (match (list-get qas k) ((some a) a) ((none) arena)))
                (share (bucket-items scratch (map-get (. buckets by-owner) k))))
            (do
              (list-push qss qs)
              ;; `share`, `ca`, `qa` and `qs` are IMMUTABLE bindings, captured
              ;; by value (slop#193: slop 0.4.0 rejects a `mut` capture in spawn).
              (if (<= w 1)
                (do (commit-bucket qa ca sat share qs) (do))
                (list-push threads
                  (spawn scratch (fn () (commit-bucket qa ca sat share qs)))))
              (set! k (+ k 1)))))
        (for-each (t threads) (join t))
        (let ((new-queues (map-new arena Node Queue))
              (new-active (set-new arena Node))
              (mut count 0))
          (do
            (for-each (qs qss)
              (for-each ((x q) (. qs by-node))
                (do
                  (map-put new-queues x q)
                  (set-put new-active x)
                  (set! count (+ count 1)))))
            (record-new Saturation
              (contexts     (. sat contexts))
              (queues       new-queues)
              (active       new-active)
              (active-count count)
              (iteration    (+ (. sat iteration) 1))))))))

  ;; What a round's join leaves for the barrier: the deltas, and the
  ;; worker arenas that hold them, freed once the commit has read them.
  (type Joined (record
    (deltas (List RoundDelta))
    (arenas (List Arena))))

  ;; A round's result, and the arenas its queues and frontier live in:
  ;; the caller frees those once the NEXT round has committed.
  (type Advanced (record
    (saturation Saturation)
    (arenas     (List Arena))))

  (fn generation-arenas ((arena Arena) (w Int))
    (@intent "A round's queue arenas: the generation arena, plus one per committer at W > 1")
    (@spec ((Arena Int) -> (List Arena)))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; The FIRST is the generation arena (the queue and frontier maps; at
    ;; W = 1 the only committer's queues too). A committer's arena is its
    ;; own because an arena is not safe to allocate from on two threads.
    ;; 1 MB to start, as the worker arenas: a busy one grows by chaining.
    (let ((mut out (list-new arena Arena)))
      (do
        (list-push out (arena-new 1048576))
        (when (> w 1)
          (let ((mut k 0))
            (while (< k w)
              (do
                (list-push out (arena-new 1048576))
                (set! k (+ k 1))))))
        out)))

  (fn committer-arenas ((arena Arena) (gen (List Arena)))
    (@intent "The committers' queue arenas: all but the generation arena, or none at W = 1")
    (@spec ((Arena (List Arena)) -> (List Arena)))
    (@alloc arena)
    (let ((mut out (list-new arena Arena))
          (mut i 0))
      (do
        (for-each (a gen)
          (do
            (when (> i 0) (list-push out a))
            (set! i (+ i 1))))
        out)))

  (fn advance-round ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (idx RuleIndex) (config ReasonerConfig)
                     (cas (List Arena)))
    (@intent "Execute one barrier-synchronized round: join read-only, then commit")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation RuleIndex ReasonerConfig (List Arena)) -> Advanced))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; ONE ROUND, EXACTLY, from `commit-round`'s own @post: slop's verifier
    ;; assumes a callee's @post where the call runs. `saturate`'s budget
    ;; invariant rests on this.
    (@post {(. (. $result saturation) iteration) == ((. sat iteration) + 1)})
    ;;
    ;; THE JOIN ALLOCATES IN A SCRATCH ARENA, FREED AT THE BARRIER. The
    ;; round's delta is a map of contexts, not needed once the round
    ;; commits, so the join and its RoundDeltas allocate in `scratch` (or a
    ;; worker's arena). Each rule call's result list is shorter-lived still:
    ;; `join-context` empties it with its context. The
    ;; commit writes the store in the run arena and the commit arenas
    ;; `cas`, and the next round's queues and frontier in this round's
    ;; generation arenas, which the caller frees a round later.
    ;;
    ;; Why nothing escapes: commit copies every fact into sets and queues
    ;; it allocates itself, and a copied Node or RoleId holds only an id or
    ;; an IRI whose bytes live in the parse arena — no rule creates a
    ;; string. `make test-asan` is what enforces this: a store that kept a
    ;; pointer into `scratch` is a use-after-free there.
    ;;
    ;; FREED EXPLICITLY. This body was a `with-arena` whose value was the
    ;; commit, and slop then lowered that to `return ...` BEFORE the free:
    ;; every round's scratch leaked, and the ASan check above never ran (M1
    ;; slice 6b C0; fixed upstream in slop #213). The explicit form stays -
    ;; it says what happens, in order.
    (let ((scratch (arena-new 16777216))
          (joined  (if (<= (. config worker-count) 1)
                     (let ((d (record-new RoundDelta (pending (map-new scratch Node Context))))
                           (mut ds (list-new scratch RoundDelta)))
                       (do
                         (round-join scratch sat idx d)
                         (list-push ds d)
                         (record-new Joined (deltas ds) (arenas (list-new scratch Arena)))))
                     (parallel-join scratch sat idx (. config worker-count))))
          ;; The generation's arena list is in the RUN arena: the caller
          ;; frees the arenas it names, so it must not live in one of them.
          (gen     (generation-arenas arena (cast Int (list-len cas))))
          (g       (match (list-get gen 0) ((some a) a) ((none) arena)))
          (next    (commit-round g arena scratch sat (. joined deltas) cas
                     (committer-arenas scratch gen))))
      (do
        (for-each (a (. joined arenas)) (arena-free a))
        (arena-free scratch)
        (record-new Advanced (saturation next) (arenas gen)))))

  (fn parallel-join ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (idx RuleIndex) (w Int))
    (@intent "The join on up to w workers, each over a share of the frontier, into its own delta")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation RuleIndex Int) -> Joined))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; WITHIN A ROUND, WORK DISTRIBUTION IS FREE (SPEC §6.8): the store is
    ;; frozen and each worker writes only its own arena and RoundDelta.
    ;; The deltas are NOT merged: `commit-round` reads them all, one
    ;; committer per context, so the round — Δₙ₊₁, the frontier, the count
    ;; — is the same at every worker count, and so is every report, capped
    ;; runs included (M1 (e)).
    ;;
    ;; The frontier is split into CONTIGUOUS CHUNKS, each list complete
    ;; before it is stored: a `(List T)` is a value struct, so round-robin
    ;; pushes into lists held in a list would silently lose writes. Thread
    ;; structs and closure environments live in `arena` (the coordinator's
    ;; scratch), which outlives every `join`; worker arenas are returned
    ;; with the deltas and freed only after the commit has read them.
    (let ((nodes   (set-elements (. sat active)))
          (n       (cast Int (list-len nodes)))
          (mut arenas  (list-new arena Arena))
          (mut deltas  (list-new arena RoundDelta))
          (mut threads (list-new arena (Ptr (Thread Int))))
          (mut k 0))
      (do
        (while (< k w)
          (let ((lo (/ (* k n) w))
                (hi (/ (* (+ k 1) n) w)))
            (do
              (when (< lo hi)
                ;; 1 MB to start, not the coordinator's 16: an arena chains
                ;; a larger block when it fills, so a busy worker grows and
                ;; an idle one costs little — 64 workers on a small frontier
                ;; asked for a gigabyte every round at 16 MB each.
                (let ((wa (arena-new 1048576))
                      (mut part (list-new arena Node))
                      (mut i lo))
                  (do
                    (while (< i hi)
                      (do
                        (match (list-get nodes i)
                          ((some x) (list-push part x))
                          ((none)   (do)))
                        (set! i (+ i 1))))
                    ;; `share` is an IMMUTABLE copy of the finished partition,
                    ;; and that is load-bearing: a closure captures a `mut`
                    ;; local BY REFERENCE (`&part` in the C), and `part` is a
                    ;; stack slot of this loop body. Spawned on `part` itself
                    ;; (which slop 0.4.0 now rejects at compile time), every
                    ;; worker read a slot the next iteration reused —
                    ;; wrong partitions, lost conclusions, the litmus failing
                    ;; deterministically. An immutable binding is captured by
                    ;; value, so each thread owns its list header (slop#193).
                    (let ((d     (record-new RoundDelta (pending (map-new wa Node Context))))
                          (share part))
                      (do
                        (list-push arenas wa)
                        (list-push deltas d)
                        (list-push threads
                          (spawn arena (fn () (round-join-part wa sat idx d share)))))))))
              (set! k (+ k 1)))))
        (for-each (t threads) (join t))
        (record-new Joined (deltas deltas) (arenas arenas)))))

  ;; ============================================================
  ;; INITIALIZATION (§6.3 step 4)
  ;; ============================================================

  (fn seed-node ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (n Node))
    (@intent "One context per signature node: empty store, both seeds in the DELTA")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation Node) -> Bool))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; Takes the whole `Saturation` because a Map may not be a parameter
    ;; type. That costs nothing: every collection field is a pointer, so
    ;; writes land in the caller's record. Only `active-count` — a
    ;; scalar — has to be tallied by the caller and written once.
    ;; Returns whether a context was actually added, so the caller can
    ;; keep `active-count` exact without a second pass.
    (if (map-has (. sat contexts) n)
      false
      (let ((ctx (make-context arena n))
            (q   (make-queue arena))
            (top (node-top)))
        (do
          ;; Δ₀(N) := { derived-sub(N), derived-sub(⊤) }, and S₋₁(N) := ∅.
          ;; The seeds go in the QUEUE, because writing them ONLY into
          ;; `subsumers` would leave every queue empty, so `active` would
          ;; be empty at round 0, the driver would report `fixpoint`
          ;; before firing a single rule, and the run would return an
          ;; instant, empty, apparently COMPLETE classification (§5.2).
          ;;
          ;; THEY GO IN THE STORE AS WELL, and that is not a
          ;; contradiction of §6.3 step 4's "empty `subsumers` set". That
          ;; sentence describes S₋₁. What this function returns is the
          ;; state at the START OF ROUND 0, which §6.4 defines as
          ;; S₀ = S₋₁ ∪ Δ₀ = Δ₀ — and in this driver the store IS the
          ;; frozen snapshot, because Δ is committed at the previous
          ;; barrier. Initialization is that barrier.
          ;;
          ;; Omitting the store write is silent and total: S(N) would be
          ;; empty throughout round 0, so CR2 could never find the OTHER
          ;; conjunct of a `sub-and` and CR4 could never satisfy B ∈ S(Y)
          ;; against a seed. The §3.1 litmus needs exactly that — CR4
          ;; joins the arriving edge against `Person ∈ S(Person)`, which
          ;; is a SEED — so `Mother ⊑ Parent` would never be derived and
          ;; the engine would look like a materializer again.
          (list-push (. q items) (union-new Derived derived-sub n))
          (list-push (. q items) (union-new Derived derived-sub top))
          (set-put (. ctx subsumers) n)
          (set-put (. ctx subsumers) top)
          (map-put (. sat contexts) n ctx)
          (map-put (. sat queues) n q)
          (set-put (. sat active) n)
          true))))

  (fn make-initial-saturation ((arena Arena) (signature (List Node)))
    (@intent "§6.3 step 4: build the context registry from the input SIGNATURE")
    (@spec ((Arena (List Node)) -> Saturation))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; Keyed on the signature rather than on occurrence-in-an-axiom,
    ;; which is what gives a DECLARED-BUT-UNUSED class a context at all:
    ;; `:C a owl:Class .` with no other mention contributes no axiom, and
    ;; an axiom-driven registry would never create `class-node(:C)` — so
    ;; HOWL could not answer `C ⊑ ⊤`, could not list C in a hierarchy,
    ;; and could not serve a probe naming it.
    ;;
    ;; ⊤ and ⊥ are added here rather than left to the caller. §6.3 step 1
    ;; pre-populates the signature with the OWL built-ins, and two
    ;; downstream claims depend on it being true unconditionally: Δ₀ is
    ;; never empty (so `max-iterations = 0` always reports
    ;; resource-limit, per `budget-exhausted`), and CR5 has a ⊥ context
    ;; to propagate through. Making it a caller obligation would leave
    ;; both silently false for a hand-built fixture.
    (@post {(. $result iteration) == 0})
    (let ((acc (record-new Saturation
                 (contexts     (map-new arena Node Context))
                 (queues       (map-new arena Node Queue))
                 (active       (set-new arena Node))
                 (active-count 0)
                 (iteration    0)))
          (mut count 0))
      (do
        (for-each (n signature)
          (when (seed-node arena acc n)
            (set! count (+ count 1))))
        (when (seed-node arena acc (node-top))
          (set! count (+ count 1)))
        (when (seed-node arena acc (node-bottom))
          (set! count (+ count 1)))
        ;; Rebuilt rather than mutated: `active-count` is a scalar, so a
        ;; write through `acc` would land in this frame's copy only.
        (record-new Saturation
          (contexts     (. acc contexts))
          (queues       (. acc queues))
          (active       (. acc active))
          (active-count count)
          (iteration    0)))))

  ;; ============================================================
  ;; THE LOOP
  ;; ============================================================

  (fn frontier-is-empty ((sat Saturation))
    (@intent "The global fixpoint is reached when no context has anything in its inbox")
    (@spec ((Saturation) -> Bool))
    (@pure)
    ;; Reading the count rather than `(list-len (set-elements ...))`
    ;; buys two things: no per-round allocation of the whole frontier
    ;; array, and a test the WP checker can actually SEE — `set-elements`
    ;; is uninterpreted, an `(Int 0 ..)` field is not.
    (@pre  {(. sat active-count) >= 0})
    (@post {$result == ((. sat active-count) == 0)})
    (== (. sat active-count) 0))

  (fn budget-exhausted ((iteration Int) (max-iterations Int))
    (@intent "Whether the permitted number of completed rounds has been used up")
    (@spec ((Int Int) -> Bool))
    (@pure)
    ;; `max-iterations = n` permits n COMPLETED global rounds.
    ;; The @pre restates Saturation's `(Int 0 ..)` iteration. That looks
    ;; redundant and is not: the WP checker does not carry range
    ;; constraints from a record field declaration into the proof, so
    ;; without it the zero-budget property below is unprovable.
    ;;
    ;; It takes the two counts, not the Saturation and the config, so
    ;; `saturate`'s budget invariant proves: slop's loop analysis cannot
    ;; follow a call inside the loop that is handed the Saturation.
    (@pre  {iteration >= 0})
    (@post {$result == (iteration >= max-iterations)})
    ;; The budget-zero case, pinned as its own obligation because it is
    ;; the boundary two conforming implementations most easily disagree
    ;; on: max-iterations = 0 performs NO rounds and must always report
    ;; the run incomplete. Δ₀ is never empty — `make-initial-saturation`
    ;; guarantees ⊤ and ⊥ contexts and seeds every context with two delta
    ;; facts — so there is no reachable empty-frontier case at budget zero.
    (@post (implies {max-iterations == 0} {$result == true}))
    (>= iteration max-iterations))

  (fn commit-arenas ((arena Arena) (w Int))
    (@intent "One arena per committer: the run arena alone at W = 1, W fresh ones otherwise")
    (@spec ((Arena Int) -> (List Arena)))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; The store grows into these for the rest of the run and extraction
    ;; reads it afterwards, so they are never freed - like the run arena.
    ;; At W = 1 the run arena is the only committer's, and nothing is
    ;; spawned: the sequential reference path.
    (let ((mut out (list-new arena Arena)))
      (do
        (if (<= w 1)
          (list-push out arena)
          (let ((mut k 0))
            (while (< k w)
              (let ((a (arena-new 1048576)))
                (do
                  (list-push out a)
                  (set! k (+ k 1)))))))
        out)))

  (fn saturate ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (idx RuleIndex) (config ReasonerConfig))
    (@intent "Run barrier-synchronized semi-naive saturation to fixpoint or the round cap")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation RuleIndex ReasonerConfig) -> (Result RoundResult Fault)))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; THE BUDGET BOUNDARY IS PINNED, because leaving it to "the test
    ;; decides" lets two conforming implementations return different
    ;; exit codes AND different report hashes at the same budget:
    ;;
    ;;   - The fixpoint check happens at a round boundary, BEFORE
    ;;     consuming budget for the next round. So if the frontier
    ;;     empties on the final permitted round the result is
    ;;     `fixpoint`, NOT `resource-limit` — reaching the answer
    ;;     exactly on budget is success.
    ;;   - `max-iterations = 0` performs no rounds and ALWAYS yields
    ;;     `resource-limit`, since Δ₀ is never empty.
    ;;
    ;; THE BUDGET BOUND, stated as the `@loop-invariant` below and proved
    ;; with the @post that rests on it. §7 calls termination for v0
    ;; "expressible as a monotone-growth-toward-a-finite-cap loop
    ;; invariant" — this is that invariant. It holds: the loop advances
    ;; only while `budget-exhausted` is false, and each `advance-round`
    ;; adds exactly one round (its proved @post). It is written for slop's
    ;; loop analysis, which does not follow a `c-inline` in the body
    ;; (slop #280: hence `cancel-requested`), a `break`, or a call handed
    ;; the Saturation (hence `budget-exhausted` takes the two counts). The
    ;; @pre only states what the @post always required: a Saturation already
    ;; past the cap could never return within it. HOWL's callers start from
    ;; iteration 0.
    (@pre {(. sat iteration) <= (. config max-iterations)})
    (@post (match $result
             ((ok r) {(. (. r saturation) iteration) <= (. config max-iterations)})
             ((error _) true)))
    ;;
    ;; A ROUND'S QUEUES ARE FREED ONE ROUND LATER. Round n's queues and
    ;; frontier (its generation's arenas) are what round n+1 joins over,
    ;; and dead once round n+1 has committed its own. Δ₀'s live in the run
    ;; arena, from initialization, and the last generation is kept: the
    ;; returned saturation holds its queues.
    (let ((mut state sat)
          (mut cancelled false)
          (mut going (not (frontier-is-empty sat)))
          (mut prev (list-new arena Arena))
          (cas (commit-arenas arena (. config worker-count))))
      (do
        ;; Fixpoint first, budget second — in that order.
        (while going
          (@loop-invariant {(. state iteration) <= (. config max-iterations)})
          (do
            ;; Cancellation is observed HERE, at the barrier, BEFORE the
            ;; budget test — a cancelled run must not be reported as
            ;; resource-limited, because the two differ in what the
            ;; consumer may conclude (§6.8). It yields Fault::cancelled
            ;; with no payload: a cancelled partial state stops at a
            ;; scheduler-dependent point and so is not reproducible.
            (when (cancel-requested (. config cancel-ptr)) (set! cancelled true))
            (if (if cancelled true (budget-exhausted (. state iteration) (. config max-iterations)))
              (set! going false)
              (let ((adv (advance-round arena state idx config cas)))
                (do
                  (for-each (a prev) (arena-free a))
                  (set! prev (. adv arenas))
                  (set! state (. adv saturation))
                  (set! going (not (frontier-is-empty state))))))))
        (if cancelled
          (error (union-new Fault cancelled))
          (if (frontier-is-empty state)
            (ok (record-new RoundResult
                  (saturation  state)
                  (termination (union-new Termination fixpoint))))
            (ok (record-new RoundResult
                  (saturation  state)
                  (termination (union-new Termination resource-limit (. state iteration))))))))))
  (fn deliver-seed ((arena Arena) (sat Saturation) (to Node) (d Derived))
    (@intent "Place one derived fact into Δ₀ for an existing context")
    (@spec ((Arena Saturation Node Derived) -> Bool))
    (@alloc arena)
    ;; §6.3 step 4: initialization IS the barrier, so asserted edges and
    ;; the range facts they carry go into Δ₀ rather than into the store.
    ;; Writing them into `subsumers` instead would make them invisible
    ;; to round 0's rules — the same failure `seed-node` documents from
    ;; the other side, where omitting the STORE write leaves CR2 and CR4
    ;; unable to see the seeds.
    ;;
    ;; Returns false when the target has no context, which means the
    ;; caller left a node out of the signature. It is not silently
    ;; created here: a context minted behind the caller's back would
    ;; make the registry a function of axiom occurrence again, which is
    ;; exactly what keying on the signature exists to avoid.
    (match (map-get (. sat queues) to)
      ((none) false)
      ((some q)
        (let ((mut qq q)
              (store (ensure-context arena sat to)))
          (do
            ;; BOTH THE STORE AND THE QUEUE, exactly as `round-commit`
            ;; writes them. Writing only the queue is silent and total:
            ;; the fact is delivered as a message for round 0, but
            ;; S(target) never contains it, so CR2 can never find the
            ;; OTHER conjunct of a `sub-and` against a seed. The
            ;; mandatory identity fixture fails precisely there — both
            ;; ranges arrive at `individual(b)` as messages, neither is
            ;; in S(b), the disjointness never fires, and the run
            ;; reports COHERENT. `seed-node` documents the same hazard
            ;; from the other side.
            (match d
              ((derived-sub b)    (set-put (. store subsumers) b))
              ((derived-succ r y) (add-succ arena store r y))
              ((derived-pred r x) (add-pred arena store r x)))
            (list-push (. qq items) d)
            (map-put (. sat queues) to qq)
            (set-put (. sat active) to)
            true)))))
)
